  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 314 AM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nThe southerly mid-level flow pattern will persist across much of\nour forecast area on Wednesday between ridging to our east and\npersistent troughing over and just west of the Baja region. Deep\nmoisture continue spread northward over our western zones within\nthis flow pattern will continue to favor a high chance (60-80%) of\nshowers and thunderstorms and an associated heavy rainfall/flash\nflood potential over southeast New Mexico, the Guadalupe and \nDavis Mountains, and the adjacent Van Horn Corridor. Other\nisolated to scattered showers/storms (20-50%) will develop across\nmuch of the rest of the forecast area Wednesday into Wednesday\nnight. Medium range models keep a moist southwesterly flow pattern\naloft in place across much of our CWA Thursday and Friday, so a\ngood chance of showers and thunderstorms will continue, especially\nover southeast New Mexico and areas along and west of the Pecos\nRiver through the latter part of the week. Deep moisture will\nsupport a continued heavy rainfall and an associated potential for\nflash flooding over our western zones Thursday into Friday.\nTemperatures will be closer to seasonal normals (though still \nslightly above what is expected for late September) mid to late \nweek. The flow pattern transitions more zonal by this weekend, \nwith weak ridging building overhead by Sunday. This should allow \nfor lower chances of showers and thunderstorms this weekend, while\ntemperatures also trend warmer back into the upper 80s to lower \n90s over much of the region.",
